   301	\section{Failure Mode Analysis}
   302	\label{app:failure-modes}
   303	
   304	The most common failure mode for the internal strategy is state hallucination: the model predicts that an action succeeded (e.g., ``the water is now boiling'') when the environment state did not change, leading to cascading errors that trigger agent death. The deterministic strategy avoids this by receiving ground-truth observations, but still dies in 16.3\% of episodes, primarily from selecting inherently dangerous actions (e.g., eating toxic substances) rather than from state misestimation.
