To test whether the death-avoidance finding generalizes beyond Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, we replicate the counterfactual analysis with Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on 7 task types ($N{=}70$ episode pairs, 10 episodes per task). The results are strongly consistent with the Qwen findings: 24 episodes (34.3\%) are deterministic-preferred, \textbf{0} (0\%) are internal-preferred, and 46 (65.7\%) show no preference. The internal strategy dies in 58.6\% of episodes versus 24.3\% for deterministic (death-rate differential $+34.3\%$), compared to $+26.6\%$ for Qwen2.5-7B. Direction consistency is perfect: all 7 task types show higher internal than deterministic death rates (7/7, compared to 10/11 for Qwen). The larger death-rate differential ($+34.3\%$ vs.\ $+26.6\%$) and the absence of any internal-preferred episodes (0 vs.\ 9 for Qwen) suggest that Llama-3.1-8B is more vulnerable to hallucination-induced death, amplifying the death-avoidance effect. These results confirm that the death-avoidance dominance is not specific to the Qwen model family.


\section{DAF Sensitivity to Zero-Death Task Types}
\label{app:daf-sensitivity}

Table~\ref{tab:daf-sensitivity} reports DAF estimates with and without \texttt{find-non-living-thing}, the only task type with zero death rate across all three models.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{DAF (11 tasks)} & \textbf{DAF (10 tasks)} \\
\midrule
Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct & 90.4\% [81.4, 99.1] & 100.0\% [100.0, 100.0] \\
Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct & 88.2\% [80.0, 95.1] & 97.0\% [91.5, 100.0] \\
Gemma-2-9B-it & 89.7\% [79.5, 98.4] & 99.9\% [99.7, 100.0] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{DAF sensitivity analysis. ``10 tasks' excludes \texttt{find-non-living-thing} (the only task type with zero death rate across all models). Episode-level bootstrap 95\% CIs ($B = 10{,}000$).}
\label{tab:daf-sensitivity}
\end{table}

For the 10 task types with nonzero death rates, DAF exceeds 97\% across all three model families, confirming that the wider CIs in the full analysis reflect the mechanical effect of including a zero-death task type rather than method instability.
\section{Failure Mode Analysis}
\label{app:failure-modes}

The most common failure mode for the internal strategy is state hallucination: the model predicts that an action succeeded (e.g., ``the water is now boiling'') when the environment state did not change, leading to cascading errors that trigger agent death. The deterministic strategy avoids this by receiving ground-truth observations, but still dies in 16.3\% of episodes, primarily from selecting inherently dangerous actions (e.g., eating toxic substances) rather than from state misestimation.

